syslogd -- System Administration

Read the system log FIFO and file what arrives
/etc/syslogd [-d] [-f conffile] [-p fifo]

syslogd is the system logger.  It reads records written by syslog(3) into the
FIFO /dev/log and appends each one to the files /etc/syslog.conf names for its
facility and level.  /etc/rc starts it; nothing else needs to.

It makes /dev/log itself, at start-up, if the node is not already there, and
sets it mode 0666 afterwards -- a log that unprivileged programs cannot write
is not a system log, and root's umask would otherwise have taken the write
bits off.  It then holds the FIFO open for reading AND writing, which is what
lets it exist before its first client does and keeps a quiet moment, when no
process has the FIFO open for writing, from looking like end of file.

A record is one write(2) of under 5120 bytes, which this kernel makes atomic,
so records from different processes cannot interleave; syslog(3) keeps one
under 256 bytes.  A record begins with its priority as `<n>', which syslogd
strips before filing; a record with no such prefix is filed as user.notice.

Options
    -d		Stay in the foreground and echo every record, with the
		facility and level it was filed under, to standard output
		as well as filing it.
    -f file	Read file instead of /etc/syslog.conf.
    -p fifo	Listen on fifo instead of /dev/log.

The Configuration File
One rule per line: a selector, white space, and the file the record is
appended to.

A selector is facility.level.  Naming a level selects that level and every
more urgent one, so mail.info takes everything from mail except debug.  `*'
stands for every facility or every level, `none' as the level takes a facility
back out again, several facilities are separated by commas and several
selectors by semicolons.  The facilities are kern, user, mail, daemon, auth,
syslog, lpr, news, uucp, cron and local0 through local7; the levels, most
urgent first, are emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info and debug.  A
line whose first non-blank character is `#' is a comment.

ONLY A FILE ACTION IS SERVED.  An action that does not begin with `/' is
skipped rather than half-honoured: there is no network here to forward a
record to and no wall(1) to write it on.  A device is a file for this purpose,
so /dev/console works, but syslogd writes it and never creates it.  At most
twelve rules are read.

A configuration that cannot be read, or that selects nothing, is not an empty
configuration: syslogd falls back to one rule sending everything to
/usr/adm/syslog.  A machine whose log vanished over a typo is worse off than
one that over-files.

Each destination is opened and closed once per record.  This machine has
twenty descriptors, the log is written a few times a minute, and a file held
open across a truncation would go on writing at the old offset.

Signals
SIGHUP rereads the configuration and files a record saying so.  SIGTERM --
which is signal 5 on this system, not 15 -- files a closing record and exits.
Both arrive while syslogd is asleep in a read of the FIFO, so each handler
only sets a flag and the work is done when the read returns.

syslogd files its own start, reread and exit records under the syslog
facility, through the same rules as everything else, so they appear where
every other record appears.

Files
/dev/log		the FIFO, made by syslogd
/etc/syslog.conf	the configuration
/usr/adm/syslog		the usual destination, and the fallback

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it: this system has a system
log and the release the Lexicon documents has none.  syslogd, syslog(3) and
logger(1) were all written for this port, and this page was written here from
syslogd's source; none of Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

There is no forwarding to another host, no /dev/klog and no mark interval.

